one for your case."}, {"id": "q9", "kind": "code", "topic": "GroupBy multiple aggs", "text": "For each `customer_id`, return both sum and mean of `amount`. Use .agg.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.groupby('customer_id')['amount'].agg(['sum', 'mean'])", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.groupby\s*\(\s*['\"]customer_id['\"]\s*\)\s*\[\s*['\"]amount['\"]\s*\]\.agg\s*\(\s*\[\s*['\"]sum['\"]\s*,\s*['\"]mean['\"]\s*\]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": ".agg(['sum', 'mean']) returns a DataFrame with one column per aggregation. Powerful for multiple metrics at once."}, {"id": "q10", "kind": "code", "topic": "Named aggregations", "text": "For each `category`, get `total = sum(amount)` and `n = count(*)`. Use .agg with named tuples.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.groupby('category').agg(total=('amount', 'sum'), n=('amount', 'count'))", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.groupby\s*\(\s*['\"]category['\"]\s*\)\.agg\s*\(\s*total\s*=\s*\(\s*['\"]amount['\"]\s*,\s*['\"]sum['\"]\s*\)\s*,\s*n\s*=\s*\(\s*['\"]amount['\"]\s*,\s*['\"]count['\"]\s*\)\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "Named aggregations (pandas 0.25+) let you name output columns directly. Best modern syntax for multi-agg."}, {"id": "q11", "kind": "code", "topic": "Apply to group", "text": "Apply a custom function to each group: return max-min spread of `amount`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.groupby('category')['amount'].apply(lambda s: s.max() - s.min())", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.groupby\s*\(\s*['\"]category['\"]\s*\)\s*\[\s*['\"]amount['\"]\s*\]\.apply\s*\(\s*lambda\s+s\s*:\s*s\.max\s*\(\s*\)\s*-\s*s\.min\s*\(\s*\)\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": ".apply runs a function per group. Slower than built-in aggregations — use built-ins (sum, mean) when possible."}, {"id": "q12", "kind": "code", "topic": "Value counts", "text": "Count unique values in column `country` of `df`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df['country'].value_counts()", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\s*\[\s*['\"]country['\"]\s*\]\.value_counts\s*\(\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": ".value_counts() = "how many of each value." Sorted descending by count. Standard first-look at a categorical column."}, {"id": "q13_d2", "kind": "code", "topic": "GroupBy top per group", "text": "For each `customer_id`, get the row with the max `amount`. Use .idxmax + .loc.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.loc[df.groupby('customer_id')['amount'].idxmax()]", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.loc\s*\[\s*df\.groupby\s*\(\s*['\"]customer_id['\"]\s*\)\s*\[\s*['\"]amount['\"]\s*\]\.idxmax\s*\(\s*\)\s*\]", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": ".idxmax returns the index of the max within each group. .loc[indexes] then fetches the actual rows. "Top 1 per group" pattern."}, {"id": "q14", "kind": "code", "topic": "Write CSV", "text": "Save `df` to 'out.csv' without the row index.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.to_csv('out.csv', index=False)", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.to_csv\s*\(\s*['\"]out\.csv['\"]\s*,\s*index\s*=\s*False\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "index=False suppresses the row index column. ALWAYS set it (otherwise CSV has an unnamed first column)."}, {"id": "q15", "kind": "code", "topic": "GroupBy then reset", "text": "Group `df` by `category` and sum, then reset_index so the group key becomes a column.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.groupby('category').sum().reset_index()", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.groupby\s*\(\s*['\"]category['\"]\s*\)\.sum\s*\(\s*\)\.reset_index\s*\(\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "After groupby+agg, group key is the index. reset_index = move it back to a column. Useful for tools that want a flat table."}], "CR-PYINT/ch5/s3": [{"id": "q1", "kind": "code", "topic": "Merge on key", "text": "Merge DataFrames `orders` and `customers` on column `customer_id`, inner join. Assign to `merged`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "merged = orders.merge(customers, on='customer_id')", "accept": [{"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*orders\.merge\s*\(\s*customers\s*,\s*on\s*=\s*['"]customer_id['"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}, {"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*pd\.merge\s*\(\s*orders\s*,\s*customers\s*,\s*on\s*=\s*['"]customer_id['"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "df.merge() is pandas' SQL JOIN. Default is inner. Use how='left'/'right'/'outer' for other types."}, {"id": "q2", "kind": "code", "topic": "Left merge", "text": "Same as before but LEFT join (keep all orders even unmatched).", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "merged = orders.merge(customers, on='customer_id', how='left')", "accept": [{"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*orders\.merge\s*\(\s*customers\s*,\s*on\s*=\s*['"]customer_id['"]\s*,\s*how\s*=\s*['"]left['"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "Left join keeps all left rows, fills NaN for unmatched right columns. Same semantics as SQL LEFT JOIN."}, {"id": "q3", "kind": "code", "topic": "concat vertical", "text": "Stack DataFrames `df1` and `df2` vertically (row-wise). Assign to `combined`.", "language": "python", "starter": "import pandas as pd
", "solution": "combined = pd.concat([df1, df2])", "accept": [{"__rx": "combined\s*=\s*pd\.concat\s*\(\s*\[\s*df1\s*,\s*df2\s*\]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}, {"__rx": "combined\s*=\s*pd\.concat\s*\(\s*\[\s*df1\s*,\s*df2\s*\]\s*,\s*axis\s*=\s*0\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "pd.concat() with a list stacks vertically by default (axis=0). axis=1 stacks horizontally (assuming aligned indexes)."}, {"id": "q4", "kind": "code", "topic": "pivot", "text": "From `df` (columns: date, product, sales), pivot so each row is a date and each column is a product, values are sales. Assign to `wide`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "wide = df.pivot(index='date', columns='product', values='sales')", "accept": [{"__rx": "wide\s*=\s*df\.pivot\s*\(\s*index\s*=\s*['"]date['"]\s*,\s*columns\s*=\s*['"]product['"]\s*,\s*values\s*=\s*['"]sales['"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "pivot() reshapes long → wide. For aggregation during the pivot (when duplicates exist), use pivot_table."}, {"id": "q5_d2", "kind": "code", "topic": "Inner merge", "text": "Inner-merge `orders` and `customers` on `customer_id`. Assign to `merged`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "merged = orders.merge(customers, on='customer_id')", "accept": [{"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*orders\.merge\s*\(\s*customers\s*,\s*on\s*=\s*['\"]customer_id['\"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "Default how='inner' — keep matches on both sides. on= when both have the same key name."}, {"id": "q6", "kind": "code", "topic": "Left merge", "text": "Left-merge `orders` (left) with `customers` on `customer_id`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "merged = orders.merge(customers, on='customer_id', how='left')", "accept": [{"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*orders\.merge\s*\(\s*customers\s*,\s*on\s*=\s*['\"]customer_id['\"]\s*,\s*how\s*=\s*['\"]left['\"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "how='left' keeps all orders, fills NaN for customers without a match. Same as SQL LEFT JOIN."}, {"id": "q7", "kind": "code", "topic": "Merge with different keys", "text": "Merge `df1.user_id` to `df2.id`. Use left_on and right_on.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "merged = df1.merge(df2, left_on='user_id', right_on='id')", "accept": [{"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*df1\.merge\s*\(\s*df2\s*,\s*left_on\s*=\s*['\"]user_id['\"]\s*,\s*right_on\s*=\s*['\"]id['\"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "Use left_on/right_on when join columns have different names. on= when they match."}, {"id": "q8", "kind": "code", "topic": "Concat vertical", "text": "Concatenate `df1` and `df2` vertically (stack rows). Use pd.concat.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "result = pd.concat([df1, df2], ignore_index=True)", "accept": [{"__rx": "result\s*=\s*pd\.concat\s*\(\s*\[\s*df1\s*,\s*df2\s*\]\s*,\s*ignore_index\s*=\s*True\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "pd.concat is the row-stacking primitive (axis=0 default). ignore_index=True resets the row index after stacking."}, {"id": "q9", "kind": "code", "topic": "Concat horizontal", "text": "Concatenate `df1` and `df2` horizontally (side by side). Use axis=1.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "result = pd.concat([df1, df2], axis=1)", "accept": [{"__rx": "result\s*=\s*pd\.concat\s*\(\s*\[\s*df1\s*,\s*df2\s*\]\s*,\s*axis\s*=\s*1\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "axis=1 = add columns side by side. They align on index — mismatch fills NaN. Mostly used when DataFrames already share index."}, {"id": "q10", "kind": "code", "topic": "Pivot table", "text": "Pivot `df` so that rows = `month`, columns = `category`, values = sum of `amount`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df.pivot_table(index='month', columns='category', values='amount', aggfunc='sum')", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\.pivot_table\s*\(\s*index\s*=\s*['\"]month['\"]\s*,\s*columns\s*=\s*['\"]category['\"]\s*,\s*values\s*=\s*['\"]amount['\"]\s*,\s*aggfunc\s*=\s*['\"]sum['\"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "pivot_table = pivot + aggregation. Great for matrix-style reports. aggfunc defaults to mean."}, {"id": "q11", "kind": "code", "topic": "Melt (unpivot)", "text": "Melt `df` to long format where columns become rows. Keep `id` fixed, melt all other columns.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "long = df.melt(id_vars=['id'])", "accept": [{"__rx": "long\s*=\s*df\.melt\s*\(\s*id_vars\s*=\s*\[\s*['\"]id['\"]\s*\]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "melt = inverse of pivot. Wide → long format. Standard for tidy data analysis (each row = one observation)."}, {"id": "q12", "kind": "code", "topic": "Set index", "text": "Set column `id` as the DataFrame index.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df = df.set_index('id')", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\s*=\s*df\.set_index\s*\(\s*['\"]id['\"]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "set_index moves a column into the index. Useful for fast .loc lookups and aligning with other DataFrames."}, {"id": "q13", "kind": "code", "topic": "Merge indicator", "text": "Use indicator=True in merge to see which side each row came from.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "merged = df1.merge(df2, on='key', how='outer', indicator=True)", "accept": [{"__rx": "merged\s*=\s*df1\.merge\s*\(\s*df2\s*,\s*on\s*=\s*['\"]key['\"]\s*,\s*how\s*=\s*['\"]outer['\"]\s*,\s*indicator\s*=\s*True\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "indicator=True adds a `_merge` column ('left_only', 'right_only', 'both'). Diagnostic gold for finding mismatches."}, {"id": "q14", "kind": "code", "topic": "Drop duplicates", "text": "Drop duplicate rows from `df` based on `email`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df = df.drop_duplicates(subset=['email'])", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\s*=\s*df\.drop_duplicates\s*\(\s*subset\s*=\s*\[\s*['\"]email['\"]\s*\]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "subset= which columns to check for duplicates. Keep='first' default; keep='last' or False (drop all dupes)."}, {"id": "q15", "kind": "code", "topic": "Crosstab", "text": "Create a crosstab of `df.gender` vs `df.country`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "pd.crosstab(df['gender'], df['country'])", "accept": [{"__rx": "pd\.crosstab\s*\(\s*df\s*\[\s*['\"]gender['\"]\s*\]\s*,\s*df\s*\[\s*['\"]country['\"]\s*\]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "crosstab = quick contingency table. Counts pairs by default. pass values/aggfunc for custom aggregation."}], "CR-PYINT/ch5/s4": [{"id": "q1", "kind": "code", "topic": "Parse dates", "text": "Convert column `df['date']` to datetime in-place.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df['date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['date'])", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\s*\[\s*['"]date['"]\s*\]\s*=\s*pd\.to_datetime\s*\(\s*df\s*\[\s*['"]date['"]\s*\]\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "String → datetime conversion. After this, you can use .dt accessor for components and time math."}, {"id": "q2", "kind": "code", "topic": ".dt accessor", "text": "Add a `month` column to `df` (numeric month from datetime column `df['date']`).", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "df['month'] = df['date'].dt.month", "accept": [{"__rx": "df\s*\[\s*['"]month['"]\s*\]\s*=\s*df\s*\[\s*['"]date['"]\s*\]\.dt\.month", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": ".dt accessor gives you all the datetime components: .year, .month, .day, .hour, .dayofweek, .quarter, etc."}, {"id": "q3", "kind": "code", "topic": "Resample monthly", "text": "DataFrame `df` has a DatetimeIndex and column `amount`. Resample to monthly sums. Assign to `monthly`.", "language": "python", "starter": "", "solution": "monthly = df['amount'].resample('M').sum()", "accept": [{"__rx": "monthly\s*=\s*df\s*\[\s*['"]amount['"]\s*\]\.resample\s*\(\s*['"]m['"]\s*\)\s*\.sum\s*\(\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}, {"__rx": "monthly\s*=\s*df\.resample\s*\(\s*['"]m['"]\s*\)\s*\[\s*['"]amount['"]\s*\]\s*\.sum\s*\(\s*\)", "__fl": "i"}], "explain": "resample('M') = monthly. 'W' = weekly, 'D' = daily, 'Q' = quarterly, 'Y' = yearly. Standard pandas time bucketing."}, {"id": "q4", "topic": "Timezone trap", "text": "A common pandas timezon